# Production Android release APK evidence

Builds on rule 148 (the pin is armed) and rule 147 (relaxing a predicate
requires a blast-radius review of its siblings).

## 1. One permanent signer. Never a second one.

alias `daengtisia-clinic-release`, RSA 4096, SHA-256

    79db269b7cd38e920b80efbcf2f59142721f1e57924d3048d07a862f34fea2d9

Never regenerate it, never add a second production signer, never rotate it as
part of ordinary work. The same signer is what makes an update installable
over an existing install; a different one forces an uninstall, and uninstalling
erases the Keystore device identity and the enrolment with it.

If a signed APK's certificate does not equal the pin: **stop**. Do not change
the pin to match the artifact. That inverts the trust anchor into a mirror.

## 2. Evidence is measured out of the artifact, never out of config

The signer fingerprint in release evidence MUST be extracted from the final
signed APK (`apksigner verify --print-certs`, via `ApksignerFingerprintResolver`)
and compared against the pin in source control.

Comparing config to config is not evidence — both sides are the same claim
written twice, and it is exactly the residual the certificate-pin sprint could
not close because no signed artifact existed yet.

`artifact_sha256` is computed AFTER signing. A digest taken before signing
identifies a file nobody will ever install.

## 3. Two commits, and they are not interchangeable

    RELEASE SOURCE   the commit + tree the APK was compiled from.
                     Clean worktree, frozen, green in CI BEFORE signing.
    FINAL CANDIDATE  the evidence-only commit recording digest, signer and CI
                     run. Merged and deployed. Must pass CI again on its tree.

The manifest carries `release_source_sha` and `release_source_tree` — not
`git_commit`, which is ambiguous precisely where ambiguity is dangerous. The
tree is content-addressed and is what makes the binding tamper-evident.

An evidence commit that presents itself as the build source asserts a build
that never happened, and the signature then appears to vouch for code the
signer never saw. Never claim the APK was built from the final candidate when
the manifest was committed afterwards.

## 4. The key never enters git, CI or the VPS

No PKCS12, no keystore, no password in the repository, in a workflow, or on the
production host. The application's only key material is PUBLIC (device
attestation). Signing happens in a human ceremony on Custodian 1: swap OFF
before the vault opens, passwords typed interactively — never in argv, an
environment variable, a log, a document or a chat — vault and mapper closed
before swap is restored.

CI must be able to build and test without any of it. `pull_request_ci_may_sign`
stays false.

## 5. A release build must actually build

`isMinifyEnabled` with a `proguardFiles(...)` naming a file that is not in the
tree fails at `:app:minifyReleaseWithR8`, and the whole release governance
chain will report GO around an artifact that cannot be produced. A marker
proving a string is PRESENT is not a check that the thing it NAMES exists —
`release_proguard_files_present` is the check that closes it.

Keep the rules file minimal by verification. A blanket `-keep class com.**`
goes green faster and disables the shrinking the release build type exists to
apply. Before concluding a keep rule is unnecessary, confirm what is actually
reflective — and in particular that nothing transmitted to the server derives
from a Kotlin symbol name, because obfuscation would then break attestation in
release builds only.

## 6. An interactive REPL is never run on production

Not for diagnostics, not for `--version`, not to ask which environment you are
in. It writes ERROR records into the application log, which pins the monitoring
log signal to WATCH for 24 hours and blinds it to real errors.

This is now mechanical, because prose failed twice: `deploy:forbidden-command-check`
runs in `scripts/sprint-release.sh` BEFORE the deploy runner opens a connection,
and refuses without a bypass flag. Patterns live in `config/release_safety.php`
so the guard never contains the literal it forbids.

Scripts and runbooks are both recurrence vectors — both incidents came from
following a documented step. Use a purpose-built read-only command
(`artisan env`, `satusehat:diagnose`, `pilot:performance-snapshot`).

## 7. Building or signing unlocks nothing else

A signed APK and its evidence do NOT mean distributed, installed, enrolled,
piloted or enforced. `DEVICE_ENFORCEMENT_ACTIVE` stays false until a sprint
that explicitly decides otherwise. Reading a green release gate as permission
to install on a tablet is the misreading rules 148 and this rule both exist to
prevent.

## 8. Full Suite deferral is authorised, and is not a pass

The GLOBAL TEMPORARY FULL-SUITE POLICY is ACTIVE
(`.github/ci-policy/full-suite-policy.json`). The classifier's
`run_full_suite=required` is a RISK SIGNAL, not an authorisation; the suite is
gated by event plus policy, and `pull_request` never enabled it. Report such a
run as **DEFERRED**, never as passed. Retiring the policy is reserved for the
consolidated closure and is forbidden as a side effect of another sprint.
